1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,819
A burning candle seems to lose mass.

2
00:00:02,819 --> 00:00:04,987
A rusting iron gate gets heavier.

3
00:00:04,987 --> 00:00:08,949
And pure water, from a river
or a lab, is always the same.

4
00:00:09,349 --> 00:00:10,458
Three puzzles.

5
00:00:10,458 --> 00:00:12,915
Five old laws explain all of them.

6
00:00:13,365 --> 00:00:16,935
Aarav finds rust on his
bicycle chain, and wonders.

7
00:00:17,285 --> 00:00:19,404
Did the chain gain mass, or lose it?

8
00:00:19,404 --> 00:00:21,355
And where did the change come from?

9
00:00:21,855 --> 00:00:25,148
By the end, you will know
the five laws reactions obey.

10
00:00:25,798 --> 00:00:29,728
Over two hundred years ago, chemists
began to weigh every reaction.

11
00:00:30,078 --> 00:00:33,954
What they found became the five
laws of chemical combination.

12
00:00:34,354 --> 00:00:35,254
Law one.

13
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:36,739
Conservation of mass.

14
00:00:36,739 --> 00:00:39,782
Matter can neither be
created nor destroyed.

15
00:00:39,782 --> 00:00:43,694
Antoine Lavoisier put it forward
in seventeen eighty nine.

16
00:00:44,094 --> 00:00:46,551
Weigh a reaction in a closed flask.

17
00:00:46,551 --> 00:00:49,892
The total mass before
equals the total mass after.

18
00:00:50,292 --> 00:00:52,447
So the rusty chain is heavier.

19
00:00:52,447 --> 00:00:55,449
Iron took in oxygen
and water from the air.

20
00:00:55,449 --> 00:00:59,063
The candle seems lighter only
because its gases escape.

21
00:00:59,713 --> 00:01:00,613
Law two.

22
00:01:00,499 --> 00:01:04,405
Definite proportions, from the
French chemist Joseph Proust.

23
00:01:04,805 --> 00:01:09,429
A given compound always contains
exactly the same proportion of elements

24
00:01:09,429 --> 00:01:10,362
by weight.

25
00:01:10,762 --> 00:01:14,224
Proust tested natural and
lab made copper carbonate.

26
00:01:14,224 --> 00:01:18,279
Both had the same percentages
of copper, carbon and oxygen.

27
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:21,475
Law three needs our molecule bench.

28
00:01:21,475 --> 00:01:25,120
Hydrogen and oxygen can make
two different compounds.

29
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:30,404
In water, one oxygen atom shares
electrons with two hydrogen atoms.

30
00:01:30,804 --> 00:01:35,846
In hydrogen peroxide, two oxygen
atoms bond to each other, and each one

31
00:01:35,846 --> 00:01:37,596
also holds a hydrogen.

32
00:01:37,996 --> 00:01:39,237
Now weigh them.

33
00:01:39,237 --> 00:01:44,051
Two grams of hydrogen combine with
sixteen grams of oxygen to make eighteen

34
00:01:44,051 --> 00:01:45,292
grams of water.

35
00:01:45,692 --> 00:01:50,415
The same two grams of hydrogen combine
with thirty two grams of oxygen

36
00:01:50,415 --> 00:01:53,774
to make thirty four grams
of hydrogen peroxide.

37
00:01:54,174 --> 00:01:57,547
John Dalton saw this pattern
in eighteen oh three.

38
00:01:57,547 --> 00:02:00,182
It is the law of multiple proportions.

39
00:02:00,832 --> 00:02:02,929
Fix the hydrogen at two grams.

40
00:02:02,929 --> 00:02:06,975
Oxygen is sixteen grams in one,
and thirty two in the other.

41
00:02:07,375 --> 00:02:08,597
Pause and think.

42
00:02:08,597 --> 00:02:12,891
What is the ratio of these oxygen
masses, in small whole numbers?

43
00:02:16,091 --> 00:02:17,049
One to two!

44
00:02:17,049 --> 00:02:21,456
The masses of one element that join
a fixed mass of the other are in a

45
00:02:21,456 --> 00:02:23,156
small whole number ratio.

46
00:02:23,806 --> 00:02:26,154
Laws four and five are about gases.

47
00:02:26,154 --> 00:02:28,153
Let's build them step by step.

48
00:02:28,503 --> 00:02:29,403
Step one.

49
00:02:29,345 --> 00:02:34,001
In eighteen oh eight, Gay Lussac found
that gases react in simple volume

50
00:02:34,001 --> 00:02:37,104
ratios, at the same
temperature and pressure.

51
00:02:37,854 --> 00:02:38,754
Step two.

52
00:02:38,728 --> 00:02:43,319
One hundred millilitres of hydrogen
and fifty millilitres of oxygen give

53
00:02:43,319 --> 00:02:46,099
one hundred millilitres of water vapour.

54
00:02:46,099 --> 00:02:47,644
Two to one to two.

55
00:02:48,394 --> 00:02:49,381
Step three.

56
00:02:49,381 --> 00:02:54,458
In eighteen eleven, Avogadro said that
equal volumes of gases, at the same

57
00:02:54,458 --> 00:02:58,462
temperature and pressure, hold
equal numbers of molecules.

58
00:02:59,212 --> 00:03:00,224
Step four.

59
00:03:00,224 --> 00:03:05,714
So two hydrogen molecules and one oxygen
molecule give two water molecules.

60
00:03:05,714 --> 00:03:08,703
The oxygen molecule must split in two.

61
00:03:08,703 --> 00:03:10,261
It has two atoms.

62
00:03:11,011 --> 00:03:12,114
Remember this.

63
00:03:12,114 --> 00:03:13,701
Atoms are never lost.

64
00:03:13,701 --> 00:03:16,606
They join in simple, whole number ratios.

65
00:03:17,306 --> 00:03:19,042
Now, one worked example.

66
00:03:19,342 --> 00:03:21,218
Carbon forms two oxides.

67
00:03:21,218 --> 00:03:26,403
In carbon monoxide, twelve grams of
carbon join sixteen grams of oxygen.

68
00:03:26,403 --> 00:03:29,878
In carbon dioxide, they
join thirty two grams.

69
00:03:29,878 --> 00:03:31,421
Which law is this?

70
00:03:31,921 --> 00:03:34,487
Carbon is fixed at twelve grams in both.

71
00:03:35,237 --> 00:03:39,288
The oxygen masses are sixteen
grams and thirty two grams.

72
00:03:40,038 --> 00:03:42,578
Sixteen to thirty two is one to two.

73
00:03:42,578 --> 00:03:44,485
A small whole number ratio.

74
00:03:45,235 --> 00:03:48,270
So this is the law of
multiple proportions.

75
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:50,880
Let's line up all five laws.

76
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:53,786
Conservation of mass, Lavoisier.

77
00:03:53,786 --> 00:03:55,777
Total mass never changes.

78
00:03:56,177 --> 00:03:58,284
Definite proportions, Proust.

79
00:03:58,284 --> 00:04:03,434
Pure water always has one gram of
hydrogen for every eight grams of oxygen.

80
00:04:03,834 --> 00:04:06,007
Multiple proportions, Dalton.

81
00:04:06,007 --> 00:04:07,971
Small whole number ratios.

82
00:04:08,371 --> 00:04:10,758
Gaseous volumes, Gay Lussac.

83
00:04:10,758 --> 00:04:12,534
Simple volume ratios.

84
00:04:12,934 --> 00:04:14,121
And Avogadro.

85
00:04:14,121 --> 00:04:17,579
Equal volumes hold equal
numbers of molecules.

86
00:04:18,229 --> 00:04:19,694
Now, the exam trap.

87
00:04:20,044 --> 00:04:24,372
Some students say a burning candle
breaks the law of conservation of mass,

88
00:04:24,372 --> 00:04:26,381
because the candle gets lighter.

89
00:04:26,781 --> 00:04:29,084
But its gases escape into the air.

90
00:04:29,084 --> 00:04:33,145
Burn it in a closed flask, and
the total mass stays the same.

91
00:04:33,795 --> 00:04:35,311
Where is this asked?

92
00:04:35,311 --> 00:04:40,543
As questions to name the law behind some
data, and short mass ratio numericals.

93
00:04:41,143 --> 00:04:42,480
Exam level now.

94
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:44,632
Element X forms two oxides.

95
00:04:44,632 --> 00:04:49,255
In one, four grams of X join
one point six grams of oxygen.

96
00:04:49,255 --> 00:04:54,228
In the other, two grams of X join
one point two grams of oxygen.

97
00:04:54,228 --> 00:04:58,386
Find the ratio of oxygen
masses for a fixed mass of X.

98
00:04:58,386 --> 00:04:59,780
Pause and solve.

99
00:05:05,180 --> 00:05:06,549
The answer is B.

100
00:05:06,549 --> 00:05:07,695
Two to three.

101
00:05:08,195 --> 00:05:10,189
First fix the mass of X.

102
00:05:10,189 --> 00:05:15,311
Double the second oxide, so four grams
of X join two point four grams of

103
00:05:15,311 --> 00:05:16,211
oxygen.

104
00:05:16,538 --> 00:05:18,798
Now compare at four grams of X.

105
00:05:18,798 --> 00:05:22,169
One point six to two point
four is two to three.

106
00:05:22,669 --> 00:05:24,484
A forgot to fix X first.

107
00:05:24,484 --> 00:05:25,828
That is the trap.

108
00:05:26,428 --> 00:05:27,891
Here is your recap.

109
00:05:27,891 --> 00:05:29,394
Screenshot this card.

110
00:05:32,594 --> 00:05:35,430
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:05:35,830 --> 00:05:36,730
One.

112
00:05:36,310 --> 00:05:40,043
Why does a rusty iron chain
weigh more than the clean chain?

113
00:05:40,843 --> 00:05:41,743
Two.

114
00:05:41,406 --> 00:05:45,904
In pure water, what is the mass
ratio of hydrogen to oxygen?

115
00:05:46,704 --> 00:05:47,604
Three.

116
00:05:47,381 --> 00:05:51,088
What did Avogadro say about
equal volumes of gases?

117
00:05:51,988 --> 00:05:56,560
Next time: what is an atom, and why
does it never break in a reaction?

118
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:58,936
We find out in Dalton's atomic theory.
